\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

\subsection{Main Results}
\label{sec:main_results}

Table~\ref{tab:main_results} summarizes TSR and CAR across conditions (50 rollouts $\times$ 10 tasks per seed). Statistical comparisons use paired $t$-tests and Cohen's $d$ against the Vanilla baseline.

\textbf{Crossover interaction between normalization and gradient flow.} The four conditions crossing normalization type (BN/LN) with gradient flow (ON/OFF) reveal a crossover interaction rather than additive effects (Section~\ref{sec:mechanism}). Full OCC with BN maintains CAR comparable to Vanilla (0.797 vs.\ 0.780, $d{=}+0.69$, $p{=}0.44$). Detach OCC with BN falls 3.4\,pp below Vanilla ($p{=}0.031$, $d{=}-3.90$), the only statistically significant pairwise difference. Detach+LN (0.765) recovers 1.9\,pp of this deficit by eliminating BatchNorm interference. However, Full OCC+LN (0.743) consistently degrades CAR by 3.7\,pp below Vanilla despite maintaining gradient flow---the worst configuration tested. BN is thus beneficial when gradient flow calibrates its statistics, but harmful when gradient flow is absent; LN shows the opposite pattern. Label content does not affect outcomes: Random OCC (0.789), Depth (0.780), and Full OCC (0.797) are all within cross-seed variance ($p > 0.4$).

\textbf{Detach destabilizes optimization broadly.} Detach OCC exhibits TSR variance an order of magnitude above other conditions ($\sigma = 0.163$ vs.\ $< 0.050$): one seed collapses to TSR 0.624 with uniform degradation across all tasks, while its CAR (0.748) stays comparable to the other two seeds. The remarkably low CAR variance ($\sigma = 0.002$) despite volatile TSR ($\sigma = 0.163$) suggests deterministic convergence to a degraded safety behavior---BN interference imposes a consistent representational distortion regardless of initialization.



\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Overall results on SafeLIBERO Level~I (10 tasks). Mean $\pm$ SD across seeds; 95\% CIs via bootstrap. $^\dagger$1 seed; $^\ddagger$2 seeds. $^*$Statistically significant vs.\ Vanilla (paired $t$-test $p{<}0.05$). Cutoff conditions retain the auxiliary head forward pass post-cutoff (Detach mode).}
\label{tab:main_results}
\small
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{4pt}
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
Condition & Seeds & TSR & CAR & $95\%$ CI & $\Delta$CAR \\
\midrule
Full OCC (BN)      & 3 & 0.894 $\pm$ 0.050 & \textbf{0.797} $\pm$ 0.032 & [.770, .832] & $+$1.7 \\
Random OCC (BN)    & 3 & 0.869 $\pm$ 0.029 & 0.789 $\pm$ 0.031 & [.754, .814] & $+$0.9 \\
Vanilla       & 3 & 0.907 $\pm$ 0.009 & 0.780 $\pm$ 0.012 & [.766, .788] & ---    \\
Detach+LN     & 3 & 0.901 $\pm$ 0.016 & 0.765 $\pm$ 0.023 & [.748, .792] & $-$1.5 \\
Detach OCC (BN)$^*$    & 3 & 0.812 $\pm$ 0.163 & 0.746 $\pm$ 0.002 & [.744, .748] & $-$3.4 \\
Full OCC+LN   & 3 & 0.897 $\pm$ 0.032 & 0.743 $\pm$ 0.024 & [.718, .766] & $-$3.7 \\
\midrule
Depth             & 4 & 0.889 $\pm$ 0.042 & 0.780 $\pm$ 0.039 & [.747, .812] & 0.0 \\
Cutoff@15K$^\dagger$    & 1 & 0.912 & 0.804 & --- & $+$2.4 \\
Cutoff@25K$^\ddagger$   & 2 & 0.887 $\pm$ 0.061 & 0.724 $\pm$ 0.051 & --- & $-$5.6 \\
Cutoff@35K        & 3 & 0.933 $\pm$ 0.032 & 0.779 $\pm$ 0.017 & [.768, .798] & $-$0.1 \\
Cutoff@45K$^\dagger$    & 1 & 0.896 & 0.764 & --- & $-$1.6 \\
Reverse Cutoff$^\ddagger$ & 2 & 0.839 $\pm$ 0.072 & 0.718 $\pm$ 0.025 & --- & $-$6.2 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Normalization--Gradient Flow Interaction}
\label{sec:mechanism}

The four conditions crossing normalization type (BN/LN) with gradient flow (ON/OFF) form a $2{\times}2$ factorial (Table~\ref{tab:factorial}). A $2{\times}2$ ANOVA confirms a significant interaction ($F(1,8){=}7.511$, $p{=}0.025$, partial $\eta^2{=}0.484$, Cohen's $f{=}0.969$), while neither main effect reaches significance (gradient flow: $p{=}0.326$; normalization: $p{=}0.231$). The interaction effect is 7.3\,pp: gradient flow's effect on CAR reverses sign depending on normalization type.

\textbf{Crossover, not additive.} With BN, gradient flow improves CAR by $+$5.1\,pp (Full OCC 0.797 vs.\ Detach 0.746; simple effect $p{=}0.052$, $d{=}5.13$). With LN, gradient flow \emph{degrades} CAR by $-$2.2\,pp (Full OCC+LN 0.743 vs.\ Detach+LN 0.765; $p{=}0.26$). Neither simple effect reaches significance individually, but the large effect sizes and opposing directions confirm the crossover pattern. This rules out an additive decomposition: gradient flow benefits are BatchNorm-mediated, arising from gradient calibration of BN running statistics, not from an intrinsic regularization property of auxiliary gradients.

\textbf{Full OCC+LN is the worst configuration.} Despite maintaining gradient flow and using a normalization layer that eliminates representational drift (Section~\ref{sec:gradient_dynamics}), Full OCC+LN (0.743) falls 3.7\,pp below Vanilla---consistently across all three seeds (0.766, 0.718, 0.744). This is the strongest evidence for a representation-behavior dissociation: the configuration that best preserves pretrained representations produces the worst safety behavior.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{$2{\times}2$ factorial: normalization type $\times$ gradient flow. The 7.3\,pp interaction effect indicates a crossover: gradient flow benefits are BN-mediated.}
\label{tab:factorial}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
 & Gradient ON & Gradient OFF & $\Delta$(ON$-$OFF) \\
\midrule
BatchNorm    & Full OCC (\textbf{0.797}) & Detach OCC (0.746) & $+$5.1 \\
LayerNorm    & Full OCC+LN (0.743) & Detach+LN (0.765) & $-$2.2 \\
\midrule
$\Delta$(BN$-$LN) & $+$5.4 & $-$1.9 & interaction: 7.3 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Gradient Dynamics}
\label{sec:gradient_dynamics}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/gradient_analysis_combined.pdf}
\caption{Gradient dynamics during VLA fine-tuning (seed 42, 200 batches at 50K checkpoint; four conditions: Vanilla, Full OCC, Detach+BN, Full OCC+LN). (a)~Action loss gradient norms per encoder layer: Vanilla, Full OCC, and Random OCC are comparable, while Detach collapses to 1--13\% of Vanilla. (b)~Auxiliary loss gradient norms in the shared encoder (Detach excluded---no auxiliary gradients by design). (c)~Action--auxiliary gradient cosine similarity per layer (near zero for both Full OCC and Random OCC, confirming orthogonality). (d)~Gradient collapse ratio (Detach / Vanilla) quantifying the per-layer collapse magnitude.}
\label{fig:gradient_analysis}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:gradient_analysis} reveals a gradient collapse mechanism underlying the Detach degradation. Action loss gradient norms are comparable across Vanilla and gradient-flow conditions (0.87--1.14$\times$ Vanilla norms), ruling out gradient amplification. Detach OCC, however, exhibits catastrophic collapse: encoder gradient norms fall to 1--13\% of Vanilla across all layers (action gradient norm 0.005 vs.\ Vanilla 0.126, a 25$\times$ reduction). Per-layer analysis (Figure~\ref{fig:per_layer_combined}, Appendix) decomposes auxiliary gradient flow into amplitude and direction: Detach collapses uniformly while gradient-flow conditions maintain Vanilla-comparable norms; DINOv2 layers show $\sim$50\% conflict rate while SigLIP late layers (blocks 21--24) drop to 19--25\%, indicating cooperative alignment. Both patterns are identical for structured and random labels. The crossover interaction (Section~\ref{sec:mechanism}) reveals that gradient flow's collapse-prevention effect is BN-mediated: under LN, gradient flow degrades rather than improves CAR.

\textbf{LN induces cross-backbone gradient redistribution.} Same-seed analysis (seed 42, four conditions, 200 batches at 50K checkpoint) reveals why LayerNorm with gradient flow degrades CAR. Under Full OCC+LN, action loss gradients in DINOv2 are suppressed 3.5$\times$ relative to BN (mean norm 0.048 vs.\ 0.168), while SigLIP action gradients are amplified 2.5$\times$ (0.206 vs.\ 0.081). Conflict rates remain ${\sim}$50\% in both conditions, ruling out directional conflict as the cause. The mechanism is cross-backbone amplitude redistribution: LN normalization redistributes gradient magnitude from DINOv2 to SigLIP, disrupting the balanced allocation that BN maintains (DINOv2: 0.8$\times$ Vanilla, SigLIP: 1.0$\times$ Vanilla). This explains why Full OCC+LN degrades CAR despite preserving pretrained representations---the problem is not \emph{what} the encoders represent, but \emph{how} gradient flow allocates learning signal across them.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.75\textwidth]{figures/cka_analysis.pdf}
\caption{CKA similarity between fine-tuned and pretrained encoder features. All conditions except Detach OCC preserve pretrained structure (CKA $\geq$0.95), including Full OCC+LN---which achieves the lowest CAR despite high CKA, evidencing a representation-behavior dissociation. Detach OCC drifts catastrophically, especially in SigLIP middle layers, confirming BatchNorm as the sole cause of representational drift.}
\label{fig:cka_analysis}
\end{figure}

\textbf{CKA confirms BN as the sole cause of representational drift---but representations do not predict behavior.} CKA between fine-tuned and pretrained features (Figure~\ref{fig:cka_analysis}) shows that all non-Detach-BN conditions preserve pretrained structure (CKA $\geq$ 0.95): Vanilla, Full OCC, Random OCC, Detach+LN, and Full OCC+LN. Detach OCC alone drifts catastrophically (DINOv2: 0.539; SigLIP: 0.312). CKA analysis indicates that LN generally does not cause representational drift (s1, s2 CKA${\approx}$0.97), suggesting that the CAR degradation under Full OCC+LN operates through gradient dynamics---specifically the cross-backbone gradient redistribution described above---rather than representation corruption. Yet Full OCC+LN achieves the \emph{lowest} CAR (0.743) while Full OCC with BN achieves the highest (0.797), both at high CKA. This \emph{representation-behavior dissociation} demonstrates that preserving pretrained features is neither sufficient nor predictive of collision avoidance.

\textbf{Same-seed validation.} The cross-backbone gradient redistribution findings are confirmed by same-seed analysis (seed 42, four conditions trained identically except for normalization and gradient flow), eliminating cross-seed confounds present in earlier cross-seed comparisons.

\subsection{Additional Observations}
\label{sec:per_task}
\label{sec:temporal}

\textbf{Per-task concentration.} Seven of ten tasks achieve CAR $\geq$ 0.92 under Vanilla; overall CAR is dominated by three physically constrained tasks (T3, T5, T6; Appendix~\ref{app:per_task}). Detach is systematically worst on these hard tasks (T3: 0.11, T5: 0.05), consistent with gradient collapse exacerbating degradation where obstacle proximity is tight.

\textbf{Egocentric depth matches Vanilla.} Depth (4 seeds) achieves CAR 0.780 $\pm$ 0.039---identical to Vanilla---despite providing per-pixel geometric information. Three label types (structured BEV, random binary, egocentric depth) yield comparable CAR when gradient flow is maintained.

\textbf{Temporal dynamics (preliminary).} Cutoff@35K (3 seeds) achieves CAR 0.779, statistically indistinguishable from Vanilla ($\Delta = -$0.1\,pp), confirming that 35K steps of gradient flow fully protects collision avoidance. Surprisingly, Cutoff@25K (0.724, 2 seeds) and Reverse Cutoff (0.718, 2 seeds) fall substantially \emph{below} Vanilla. The most parsimonious explanation: setting the loss weight to zero retains the auxiliary head forward pass, so the post-cutoff period operates in Detach mode---BatchNorm interference acts on representations shaped by gradient flow, without gradient compensation. This is not withdrawal from gradient flow \emph{per se}, but transition into an actively harmful regime. Third seeds are in progress; extended analysis in Appendix~\ref{app:temporal}.

\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}
\label{sec:why_gradient}
\label{sec:why_detach}

Gradient norms, CKA, normalization ablation, and behavioral metrics converge on a crossover interaction account. BatchNorm and gradient flow are not independent factors with additive effects; they interact (7.3\,pp interaction effect). BN is a context-dependent component: when gradient flow calibrates its running statistics, BN coupling is beneficial (Full OCC 0.797, the best configuration); without gradient calibration, BN statistics drift uncompensated, causing catastrophic representational corruption (Detach OCC CKA $\downarrow$0.31--0.54). This is consistent with the known sensitivity of BatchNorm to distribution shift~\cite{ioffe2015batchnorm}, here manifested as a training-time phenomenon rather than the typical train/test discrepancy.

The crossover is most striking in the Full OCC+LN result: gradient flow with LayerNorm produces the \emph{worst} safety behavior (CAR 0.743) despite preserving pretrained representations (CKA $\geq$0.95). Same-seed gradient analysis reveals the mechanism: LN redistributes gradient magnitude across backbones, suppressing DINOv2 action gradients 3.5$\times$ while amplifying SigLIP 2.5$\times$ relative to BN, without increasing directional conflict. This cross-backbone gradient redistribution---not representation corruption---explains the CAR degradation, constituting a representation-behavior dissociation where representational fidelity does not predict safety. This rules out the hypothesis that gradient flow provides intrinsic behavioral regularization~\cite{lee2015deeply, pezeshki2021gradient}. Instead, gradient flow benefits are entirely BN-mediated: BN maintains balanced gradient allocation across encoders (DINOv2: 0.8$\times$, SigLIP: 1.0$\times$ Vanilla norms), a calibration effect that LN disrupts.

We observe that under Detach BN, SigLIP drifts more than DINOv2 (CKA 0.312 vs.\ 0.539). SigLIP's cooperative gradient alignment in late layers (19--25\% conflict) creates vulnerability when auxiliary flow is removed; DINOv2's orthogonal structure ($\sim$50\% conflict) confers resilience. LayerNorm fully resolves this asymmetry (Detach+LN: both encoders CKA $\geq$ 0.96).

\textbf{Practical guideline.} Maintain BatchNorm with gradient flow---the only configuration that preserves or improves CAR. If gradient flow cannot be maintained, substitute LayerNorm to prevent representational drift. Never combine LayerNorm with gradient flow, as this consistently degrades safety below Vanilla. No ground-truth spatial labels are required; the head is discarded at zero inference cost. This training-time intervention complements runtime safety methods~\cite{hu2025vlsa, raja2024ogmcbf}.

\textbf{Limitations.} Single architecture (OpenVLA-OFT, LoRA rank 32), single benchmark (SafeLIBERO Level~I, 10 tasks, static obstacles). The interaction effect is statistically confirmed ($F(1,8){=}7.511$, $p{=}0.025$) but based on $n{=}3$ seeds per cell; larger-scale replication would further strengthen conclusions. Gradient redistribution findings are validated by same-seed analysis (seed 42, four conditions). Cutoff conditions have 1--3 seeds, limiting temporal conclusions to preliminary observations.

