\section{Extended Per-Task Results}
\label{app:per_task}

Table~\ref{tab:per_task} reports per-task mean CAR across seeds for each condition. Seven tasks achieve CAR $\geq$ 0.92 under Vanilla, while three physically constrained tasks (T3, T5, T6) fall below 0.58. The overall CAR differences reported in the main text are dominated by these three hard tasks, which average 0.342 compared to 0.968 for the seven easy tasks.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Per-task mean CAR across seeds (50 rollouts per seed). All conditions use 3 seeds except Depth (4). Detach+LN per-task uses 2 seeds (s1, s2; s0 lacks per-task breakdown); 3-seed aggregate is in Table~\ref{tab:main_results}.}
\label{tab:per_task}
\begin{tabular}{clccccccc}
\toprule
 & & \multicolumn{7}{c}{Mean CAR by Condition} \\
\cmidrule(lr){3-9}
Task & Diff. & Vanilla & Full OCC & Random & Detach & Det+LN & Depth & Cut@35K \\
\midrule
T0 & Easy  & 0.98 & 0.98 & 0.99 & 0.97 & 0.97 & 0.99 & 0.98 \\
T1 & Easy  & 0.92 & 0.93 & 0.99 & 0.89 & 0.78 & 0.93 & 0.95 \\
T2 & Easy  & 0.92 & 0.93 & 0.94 & 0.93 & 0.90 & 0.90 & 0.94 \\
T3 & Hard  & 0.38 & 0.37 & 0.31 & \textbf{0.11} & 0.44 & 0.50 & 0.55 \\
T4 & Easy  & 0.98 & 0.99 & 0.95 & 0.98 & 0.96 & 0.92 & 0.97 \\
T5 & Hard  & 0.07 & 0.17 & 0.23 & \textbf{0.05} & 0.12 & 0.08 & 0.04 \\
T6 & Hard  & 0.57 & 0.61 & 0.55 & 0.56 & 0.36 & 0.54 & 0.38 \\
T7 & Easy  & 0.97 & 0.99 & 0.95 & 0.98 & 1.00 & 0.96 & 0.98 \\
T8 & Easy  & 1.00 & 1.00 & 1.00 & 0.99 & 0.99 & 0.99 & 0.99 \\
T9 & Easy  & 1.00 & 0.99 & 0.99 & 1.00 & 1.00 & 1.00 & 1.00 \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{2}{l}{Overall} & 0.780 & 0.797 & 0.789 & 0.746 & 0.765 & 0.780 & 0.779 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\textbf{Ceiling effects.} On the seven easy tasks (T0--T2, T4, T7--T9), all conditions achieve CAR $\geq$ 0.89, leaving minimal room for auxiliary training to improve performance. The obstacle is spatially separated from the manipulation path in these tasks.

\textbf{Hard task patterns.} On Task~5 (Vanilla CAR 0.07), gradient-flow conditions (Random OCC 0.23, Full OCC 0.17) exceed Vanilla and Detach (0.05), but all values remain below 0.23. Cutoff@35K achieves the highest T3 CAR (0.55) but the lowest T5 CAR (0.04), illustrating task-specific temporal sensitivity. Depth exhibits high cross-seed variance on hard tasks (T3 range: 0.20--0.78; T6 range: 0.16--0.84), consistent with auxiliary supervision increasing seed sensitivity.

\section{Training Details}
\label{app:training}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Training hyperparameters.}
\label{tab:hyperparams}
\begin{tabular}{ll}
\toprule
Parameter & Value \\
\midrule
Base model & OpenVLA-7B~\cite{kim2024openvla} \\
Fine-tuning recipe & OFT~\cite{kim2025oft} \\
Vision encoders & DINOv2 (1024-d) + SigLIP (1152-d) \\
Patch token dim. & 2176 (concatenated) \\
LoRA rank / $\alpha$ & 32 / 16 \\
Batch size & 8 \\
Learning rate & $5 \times 10^{-4}$ (constant) \\
Training steps & 50{,}000 \\
Aux.\ loss weight $\lambda_0$ & 0.01 \\
Aux.\ loss decay & $\exp(-t / 50{,}000)$ \\
BEV grid resolution & $64 \times 64$ \\
Focal loss $\alpha$ / $\gamma$ & 0.25 / 2.0 \\
Depth loss & Smooth-L1 \\
Aux.\ head parameters & 1.2M \\
Hardware & NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000 (98\,GB) \\
Wall time per run & $\sim$12.9 hours \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\textbf{Evaluation protocol.} Each condition uses 3+ seeds (Depth uses 4; Cutoff@25K and Reverse Cutoff use 2; Cutoff@15K and Cutoff@45K use 1) with 50 rollouts per task per seed (500 episodes per seed). A collision is registered when the L1 displacement of the obstacle's \texttt{body\_xpos} exceeds $10^{-3}$. Bootstrap 95\% CIs are computed via 10{,}000 resamples (percentile method).

\textbf{Statistical tests.} Pairwise comparisons against Vanilla use two-tailed paired $t$-tests on per-seed mean CARs with Cohen's $d$ for effect size. With $n{=}3$ seeds, statistical power is limited; we report $p$-values and effect sizes for transparency. Only Detach OCC reaches significance ($p{=}0.031$, $d{=}-3.90$); all gradient-flow conditions show $p > 0.35$.

\textbf{CKA analysis.} Centered Kernel Alignment~\cite{kornblith2019similarity} measures representational similarity between fine-tuned and pretrained encoder features. We extract patch token activations from the final layer of DINOv2 and SigLIP for 1{,}000 validation images, compute linear CKA per layer, and report the mean across layers. Higher CKA indicates better preservation of pretrained representations.

\textbf{Cutoff implementation.} Setting the auxiliary loss weight to zero at step $N$ does \emph{not} disable the auxiliary head's forward pass. The head's BatchNorm2d layers continue computing batch statistics, which shift the encoder's internal activations without compensating gradient flow. The post-cutoff phase thus operates in Detach mode, not Vanilla mode. This is a property of the implementation (the head remains in the forward graph), not a deliberate design choice.

\section{Extended Analysis}
\label{app:extended}

The Detach+LN ablation replaces BatchNorm2d with LayerNorm in the auxiliary head while retaining \texttt{stop\_gradient}. Results (3 seeds) are reported in Table~\ref{tab:main_results} and analyzed in Section~\ref{sec:why_gradient}. Detach+LN recovers 1.9\,pp of the 3.4\,pp Detach deficit (CAR 0.765 vs.\ Detach 0.746) and fully restores pretrained CKA to Vanilla level (DINOv2: 0.966, SigLIP: 0.960), confirming that BatchNorm interference accounts for all representational drift. The crossover interaction (Section~\ref{sec:mechanism}) reveals that this recovery is normalization-dependent: Full OCC+LN (gradient flow ON, LayerNorm) achieves CAR 0.743---\emph{below} Detach+LN---demonstrating that gradient flow benefits are BN-mediated rather than intrinsic.

\textbf{Per-layer gradient analysis.} Figure~\ref{fig:per_layer_combined} decomposes auxiliary gradient flow into two dimensions: amplitude (top row) and direction (bottom row). The amplitude heatmap (panels a--b) shows that Vanilla, Full OCC, and Random OCC maintain comparable action gradient norms across all layers (warm colors), while Detach collapses to 1--13\% of Vanilla uniformly across both encoders (dark), consistent with a global gradient flow disruption rather than layer-specific interference. The directional analysis (panels c--d) reveals encoder-specific alignment: DINOv2 conflict rates hover near 50\% across all layers while SigLIP late layers (blocks 21--24) drop to 19--25\%, indicating cooperative gradient alignment in semantically rich layers. Both patterns are identical for Full OCC and Random OCC, confirming they arise from architectural structure rather than label content. The aggregate conflict rates (Full OCC 44.1\%, Random OCC 43.9\%) fall below the 50\% expected for random vectors because they reflect this mixture of two encoder-specific patterns.

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/per_layer_combined.pdf}
\caption{Per-layer gradient analysis decomposed into amplitude and direction. \textbf{Top (a--b):} Action gradient norm heatmap (log scale). Gradient-flow conditions (Vanilla, Full OCC, Random OCC) maintain comparable norms; Detach collapses to 1--13\% of Vanilla across all layers. \textbf{Bottom (c--d):} Gradient conflict rate between action and auxiliary losses. DINOv2 maintains $\sim$50\% conflict (near-orthogonal), while SigLIP late layers drop to 19--25\% (cooperative alignment). Both amplitude and directional patterns are identical for Full OCC and Random OCC, confirming architectural rather than label-dependent structure.}
\label{fig:per_layer_combined}
\label{fig:conflict_rate}
\label{fig:grad_norm_heatmap}
\end{figure}

\label{app:temporal}
\textbf{Dose-response: gradient flow duration vs.\ CAR.} Figure~\ref{fig:dose_response} plots CAR against the number of OCC gradient flow steps for the cutoff series (15K--45K, plus Detach at 0K and Full OCC at 50K). The relationship is non-monotonic with substantial seed-dependent variability: Cutoff@15K (1 seed) achieves the highest CAR (0.804) despite the shortest gradient flow period, while Cutoff@25K (2 seeds) shows the lowest (0.724). Multi-seed conditions (Cutoff@35K and Full OCC) cluster near the Vanilla baseline. Reverse Cutoff (late 15K) falls below the early-cutoff counterpart at the same gradient flow duration, consistent with the continuity hypothesis. The non-monotonicity and high variance across low-seed conditions underscore that transition \emph{timing} and \emph{seed sensitivity}---not gradient flow dose alone---drive cutoff outcomes.

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.75\textwidth]{figures/dose_response.pdf}
\caption{Dose-response: CAR vs.\ OCC gradient flow duration. Blue circles: early-cutoff progression (gradient flow for the first $N$ steps, Detach mode thereafter). Red square: Reverse Cutoff (gradient flow for the last 15K steps). Gray band: Vanilla $\pm$ 1 SD. Seed counts annotated; error bars show cross-seed SD where available. The non-monotonic pattern and high variance for low-seed conditions suggest that transition timing matters more than gradient flow dose.}
\label{fig:dose_response}
\end{figure}

